{"name":"Beacon Blue","hex":"#265c98","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"beacon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A signal fire to notify of the approach of an enemy, or to give any notice, commonly of warning.","A signal, buoy, post, or other conspicuous mark erected on an eminence near the shore, or moored in shoal water, as a guide to mariners, particularly to warn vessels of danger."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To act as a beacon.","To give light to, as a beacon; to light up; to illumine."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/beacon"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Beacon","blocks":[{"text":"A beacon is an intentionally conspicuous device designed to attract attention to a specific location. A common example is the lighthouse, which draws attention to a fixed point that can be used to navigate around obstacles or into port. More modern examples include a variety of radio beacons that can be read on radio direction finders in all weather, and radar transponders that appear on radar displays."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beacon"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Beacon_at_Orontes_Bank.jpg/500px-Beacon_at_Orontes_Bank.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Beacon_at_Orontes_Bank.jpg/1280px-Beacon_at_Orontes_Bank.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Beacon_at_Orontes_Bank.jpg/500px-Beacon_at_Orontes_Bank.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A navigational beacon denoting the presence of Orontes Bank off Port Vincent, South Australia.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeacon_at_Orontes_Bank.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Culmstock%2C_Culmstock_Beacon_-_geograph.org.uk_-_213525.jpg/500px-Culmstock%2C_Culmstock_Beacon_-_geograph.org.uk_-_213525.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Culmstock%2C_Culmstock_Beacon_-_geograph.org.uk_-_213525.jpg/500px-Culmstock%2C_Culmstock_Beacon_-_geograph.org.uk_-_213525.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"16th-century beacon hut in Culmstock, Devon, England","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACulmstock%2C_Culmstock_Beacon_-_geograph.org.uk_-_213525.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/